              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The scene where Del Boy falls through the bar is widely considered one of the most iconic and funniest moments in British television history. \n\n### Episode Details\n* **Episode Title:** \"Yuppy Love\"\n* **Season/Episode:** Series 6, Episode 1\n* **Original Air Date:** 8 January 1989\n* **Writer:** John Sullivan\n\n### The Setup and the Scene\nIn the episode, Del Boy (David Jason) has adopted a \"yuppy\" image, wearing a trench coat, carrying a briefcase, and frequenting trendy London wine bars in an effort to appear modern and sophisticated. \n\nWhile in a bar with Trigger (Roger Lloyd-Pack), Del Boy spots two attractive women looking in their direction. Attempting to look incredibly smooth and impress them, he turns to Trigger and says his now-famous line:\n\n> *\"Play it nice and cool, son, nice and cool\u2014you know what I mean?\"*\n\nAs he speaks, he attempts to lean casually against the bar's counter. Unbeknownst to Del, a bartender has just lifted the bar flap to walk through. Del leans back into thin air and tumbles straight down, completely disappearing behind the bar. \n\nThe comedy is perfected by Trigger's reaction: having briefly looked away, Trigger turns back to see that Del has vanished. Instead of looking over the bar, he looks around the room with a completely bewildered expression, utterly clueless as to where his friend went.\n\n---\n\n### Behind-the-Scenes Trivia\n\n* **Trigger wasn't originally meant to be there:** A rehearsal script discovered years later revealed that Roger Lloyd-Pack\u2019s character, Trigger, was not originally in the scene. Lloyd-Pack happened to be on set that day, so writer John Sullivan decided to write him in. Trigger's puzzled reaction ended up becoming half of what made the scene so legendary.\n* **Keeping it \"Fresh\":** David Jason reportedly refused to over-rehearse the fall on the day of filming. He wanted the physical comedy to look natural and knew that performing it too many times in front of the live studio audience would ruin the genuineness of their laughter. \n* **The \"Redhead\" cue:** To time the fall perfectly, the crew used a cue. A lighting technician was instructed to adjust a light (known in the industry as a \"redhead\"). As soon as the light moved, the actor playing the bartender knew to lift the hatch, and David Jason knew to lean back, ensuring split-second comedy timing.",
